Machine learning:
  - you provide data and expected outputs, the algorithm discovers the patterns and rules
  - the "model" that comes out of training IS the rules embedded as numbers (weights, params)

Types of machine learning:
  - Supervised learning: you provide labeled data (input-output pairs) and the algorithm learns to map inputs to outputs 
  - unsupervised learning: you have inputs only, no labels. the model finds the structure on its own
  - reinforcement learning: an agent takes action in an environment and receives rewards or penalties

The ML workflow:
  - collect data: gather raw data
  - clean and explore: handle missing values, remove duplicates, visualize distributions, identify anomalies
  - feature engineering: transform the cleaned data into features the model can use. eg turn dates into day-of-week
  - split data: divid into testing, training, validation ~ 70, 15, 15 split
  - train model: feed training data into an algorithm. algorithm adjusts intern params to minimize a loss function (gradient descent)
  - evaluate: measure performance on validation/test data
  - deploy: put the model into production where it makes new predictions on new data
  - monitor: track performance over time

  